Conversion 3 of docs/research-notes/cumulative-coherence-rule-to-check-2026-08-21.md.
Two prose prohibitions become boundaries that cannot be forgotten.

Layer 1: scripts/deny_forbidden_bash_commands.py runs as a PreToolUse
Bash hook (.claude/settings.json). It denies, with the rule and the
sanctioned alternative in the message: a working-tree stash (Never-Stash
rule; safe-pull.sh / commit-first), --no-verify on a git command
(standing no-skip-hooks feedback), and the operator-only publication
commands (arXiv submit/upload, openreview, gh release create, twine
upload). Fail-open on malformed input, deliberately: a targeted boundary
on six command shapes must not block every Bash call on a harness
glitch. Known accepted FP mode: a command ABOUT a forbidden command is
denied too — observed live minutes after the hook shipped, when it
denied this session's own spec-writing heredoc; the fix is to rephrase
or route through a non-Bash tool, and the boundary held exactly as
designed.

Layer 2: _submission_verb_warnings in research_conductor.py scans task
prompts at activation and logs WARNINGs, never blocks — the honest
phrasing "do NOT run <submission command>" would trip a hard text scan
(negation blindness). operator_override (>=10 chars) clears it. Wired
in the live _activate_next_roadmap next to the exclusion-manifest call.

Evidence: 8 tests; mutations: stash rule neutered -> RED (2 tests),
override skip removed -> RED; restored GREEN. FP counts: live
research-roadmap.yaml (4 tasks) draws 0 warnings; benign command set
(git status, commit-first, safe-pull.sh, pytest, gh release list,
gh pr create, pip install twine) all pass the hook.

